{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.ComplexAlgebra","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.ComplexAlgebra","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Complex.FiniteDimensional","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.Algebra"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.mixed_eq_mul_im","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.mixed_eq_mul_im","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochRigidity/ComplexAlgebra.lean#L116-L173","doc":"**The mixed-derivative identity forces `β·|z|²|1-z|² = k·Im z`.** Substitute\n`x = (1-b)/(1-ab)`, `y = a x` (so `y/x = a`, `(1-x)/(1-y) = b`), apply `cocycle_of_mixed` with\n`X = x̄`, `Y = ȳ`, and conclude with `cocycle_eq_mul_im`. The hypothesis `h` is exactly what the\nchain rule produces from the five-term relation; the proof itself uses no analysis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.one_sub_odd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.one_sub_odd","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochRigidity/ComplexAlgebra.lean#L89-L114","doc":"**Two-term symmetry over `ℂ`.** A real five-term solution that is inversion-odd and\nconjugation-odd satisfies `φ (1 - z) = - φ z` on `ℂ ∖ {0,1}`. This is pure algebra: `two_term`\nhandles generic `z`. The exceptional point `z = 2` is real, where conjugation-oddness gives\n`φ = 0`. The exceptional roots of `z² - z + 1` satisfy `1 - z = z⁻¹`, so inversion applies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.cocycle_eq_mul_im","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.cocycle_eq_mul_im","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochRigidity/ComplexAlgebra.lean#L45-L87","doc":"**The ρ-cocycle is a multiple of `Im`.** If a real function `ρ` satisfies\n`ρ(ab) = ρ(a)·b̄ + a·ρ(b)` for all `a, b ∉ {0,1}` with `ab ≠ 1`, then `ρ = k · Im` on `ℂ ∖ {0,1}`.\nPure algebra in `ℂ`: the imaginary part of the identity gives `ρ(a) Im b = ρ(b) Im a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.rlinear_eq_add_conj","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.rlinear_eq_add_conj","kind":"theorem","line":32,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochRigidity/ComplexAlgebra.lean#L32-L43","doc":"Every `ℝ`-linear map `ℂ → ℂ` is `v ↦ α v + β v̄`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.rlinear_functional_eq_re","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BlochRigidity.rlinear_functional_eq_re","kind":"theorem","line":22,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/BlochRigidity/ComplexAlgebra.lean#L22-L30","doc":"Every `ℝ`-linear functional on `ℂ` is `v ↦ Re (ω v)` for `ω = L 1 - i L i`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}